ally  an effort to reduce the amount of opium  poppy production in afghanistan at the  practical level most units knew better  than to try  they didn't want to lose people to this  and lose a lot of people and  there was a  doctrinal  contradiction in that counter-insurgency  you're supposed to get along with the  local population but if you're attacking  the local population's wartime economy  which was often opium based  because opium unlike tomatoes or melons  is an enduring product that you can hold  on to until the road is open it's not  perishable  and so naturally in a war climate opium  was being grown very heavily in the  areas that  could grow opium uh because of climate  and water access  and the americans just left it alone or  they would engage in as the american  military often does activities that look  like accomplishment but actually are not  so you could go see opium fields being  burned  but the fields already would have been  harvested  and  and there there'd be no gunfight because  they would pay the farmer to let the  field be burned but he'd already removed  his product and you're burning the  stubble and that was a very safe patrol  to go on  it was also a farce  an absolute farce  and it was a common one  and you could also find very reliably in  the areas where opium was being grown  places where it would be almost  certainly grown and that was inside the  grounds of an afghan police station  and that's just kind of how the way it  was and this book brings that home  really well and uh and i i commend and  recommend it both  thank you there's books outside  i also want to thank everybody just  remind you there's a reception outside  so again chris chivers  angelica duran martinez stephen kinser  and of course peter andreas thank you  [Applause]  book tv continues now on c-span 2.


